      "math_academy_id": "q-77509",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/667454b0cdd06afe7b722aad8ad64423491e8e13a3eb950945af7bedcb7291c9.png)\n\nGiven the vectors $a$ and $b$ shown above, which of the following could be the linear combination $-4a-3b?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall that\n\n$$\n-4a-3b=-4a+(-3b).\n$$\n\n- The vector $-4a$ has the direction that is opposite to $a$ and $4$ times the magnitude.\n\n- The vector $-b$ has the direction that is opposite to $b$ and $3$ times the magnitude.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/fb02d818d2ad88acc13802287bc02fa194afba38ec018429b867fe5f21a6206e.png)\n\nNow, we can draw $-3b$ starting from the head of $-4a.$ The resultant \n\n$$\n-4a-3b\n$$\n\ngoes from the tail of $-4a$ to the head of $-3b.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14251898/assets/e30a16feb7f1fcc75789443db72de1fda10b61762401d19ee296df11aa03b09d.png)",
